> > > > > Hi Aniruddha,
> > > > >
> > > > > Methylation per group is calculated as weighted average of methylation
> > > > > percentages of individual samples in the same group. That means, for a
> > > > > CpG in males, the methylation percentage will be closer to the male
> > > > > with higher coverage. Weights are read coverages in this case. Alex
> > > > > Meissner's papers also employ a similar strategy. Then, for percent
> > > > > methylation difference, you simply subtract that weighted group
> > > > > average of males from females or the other way around. The direction
> > > > > of subtraction depends on what you defined as control in treatment
> > > > > vector.
> > > > >
> > > > >
> > > > > and for the statistical test employed check here:
> > > > > http://genomebiology.com/2012/13/10/R87
> > > > >
> > > > >
> > > > > Best,
> > > > > Altuna
